  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday night through Monday)\nIssued at 123 AM MDT Tue Aug 4 2026\n\nThe high pressure does not move a whole lot throughout the next \nseveral days. It meanders to center itself near AZ/UT by the latter \nhalf of this week while strengthening a decameter or two. Daily \nthunderstorm chances remain across the high terrain of the central \nmountain chain and the southwest mountains, with higher confidence \nin storms around the Gila Region. The severe thunderstorm threat \nlooks to wane across northeast NM as the flow shifts more northerly, \nthough cannot completely rule out an isolated strong storm across \nthe central mountain chain later this week. The area of high \npressure then looks to elongate once again going into the weekend \nand into next week. This may allow a few easterly waves to traverse \nits southern periphery and start to inject moisture into the region. \nEnsemble clustering suggests high confidence in this high pressure \npattern occurring, though easterly waves can be very finicky in \nterms of development and influence. The CPC has outlined a slight to \nmoderate risk of heavy rainfall for the time period of next Tuesday \nthrough Thursday, focused more on western NM into AZ. The overall \npattern does look promising for precipitation, but will need to see \nthe finer details ironed out more for confidence to increase.\n\nTemperatures look to remain near to above average across the \nforecast area for the next several days as the effects of the high \npressure are still felt. High 90s to low 100s will be common across \nlower elevation areas, and some higher elevation areas may creep \ninto the high 80s to low 90s. Temperatures may slowly decrease \nacross western NM later in the forecast period given the potential \nincrease in moisture.",
